Record the faulting frame's return address on the first-EL1-sync panel.

ELR/ESR/FAR name the faulting function, and the faults this port is chasing land inside
shared helpers such as _strlen, so ELR alone does not say which caller passed the bad
pointer.  sleh.c already holds the full arm_saved_state64_t, so keep x30 and print it
as CALLER=.

--- a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
@@ -630,12 +630,13 @@
 
 	ss_valid = is_saved_state64(ss);
 	arm_saved_state64_t *state = saved_state64(ss);
 #if SHENG
 	/* Do not pin recoverable page faults; preserve registers on screen only for confirmed fatal faults. */
 	if (ss_valid && !PSR64_IS_USER(state->cpsr)) {
+		sheng_fault_lr = state->lr;
 		sheng_log_exception(state->pc, state->esr, state->far, state->sp);
 	}
 #endif
 
 	os_atomic_cmpxchg(&original_faulting_state, NULL, state, seq_cst);
 
